Blue Team
Blue Team = defensive security.
The main goal is to monitor for attacks, detect threats, and respond quickly when something happens.
A Blue Team can have different departments and roles depending on the size of the company.

SOC — Security Operations Center
The SOC is usually the central part of an organization's cybersecurity team. It is often the first line of defense and handles security alerts and attacks.

Common SOC roles:
L1 Analyst → Junior role. Checks/triages alerts and sends complicated cases to L2.
L2 Analyst → More experienced. Investigates more advanced attacks.
Engineer → Configures and maintains security tools such as SIEM and EDR.
Manager → Manages the SOC team.

CIRT / CSIRT / CERT
If an incident is too serious or complicated for the SOC, a CIRT (Computer Incident Response Team) can handle it.
They respond to major security incidents and investigate breaches.

Specialized Blue Team Roles

Larger companies may have more specialized roles:
Digital Forensics Analyst → Investigates disks and memory to find evidence of attacks.
Threat Intelligence Analyst → Researches threat actors and emerging threats.
AppSec Engineer → Helps make software and the development process secure.
AI Researcher → Researches AI-related threats and defenses.
What I should remember

Blue Team = Defense
SOC = First line of defense
L1 → L2 → Engineer → Manager

Specialized roles require deeper knowledge and experience.
